,title,link,text,language,family
0,Kurdish folk music - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdish_folk_music,"Kurdish folk music (Kurdish: میوزیکی فۆلکلۆری کوردی, romanized: Mîwzîkî folkilorî Kurdî or مۆسیقای فۆلکلۆر) is a musical tradition associated with the Kurdish people, encompassing a variety of regional styles, instruments, and lyrical themes.","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
1,Kurdish language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdish_language,"Kurdish (Kurdî, کوردی, pronounced [kʊrdiː] ⓘ) is a Northwestern Iranian language or group of languages spoken by Kurds in the region of Kurdistan, [10][11] namely in southeast Turkey, northern Iraq, northwest Iran, and northern Syria.","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
2,Kurdish folk music — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/kurdish_folk_music,"Kurdish folk music traces its roots to the ancient Indo-European tribes that settled in northern Mesopotamia and the Iranian plateau around 4,000 years ago, forming the ethnic foundation of the Kurdish people. [5]","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
3,Kurdish music — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Kurdish_music,"Kurdish music encompasses the traditional folk and contemporary musical practices of the Kurdish people, an ethnic group dispersed across southeastern Turkey, northern Iraq, northwestern Iran, and northern Syria, characterized by oral transmission, pentatonic scales akin to but distinct from Persian modes, and themes centered on love, exile, heroism, and resistance to oppression. [1] [2 ...","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
4,"Kurdish Music | Origins, Lineage, and Sound | The Sound Atlas",https://thesoundatlas.org/discover/kurdish-music,"The genre encompasses ancient bardic traditions like the dengbej epic singers of northern Kurdistan, the complex wedding music of Iraqi Kurdistan, the revolutionary songs of the Kurdish resistance movement, and a thriving contemporary scene of pop, rock, and hip hop artists.","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
5,An Introduction to Kurdish Folk Music - FolkWorks,https://folkworks.org/article/an-introduction-to-kurdish-folk-music/,"The Kurds speak several dialects of Kurdish, an Indo-Iranian language and have lived in the region of Kurdistan for thousands of years. Western allies included a plan for an independent Kurdistan as they partitioned the Ottoman Empire after World War I.","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
6,The Music of Kurdistan - Elbphilharmonie Mediatheque,https://www.elbphilharmonie.de/en/mediatheque/the-music-of-kurdistan/898,"Kurdish languages belong to the Indo-European family of languages and, within that, to the Iranian group of languages. Kurdish is therefore not related to Arabic or Turkish, but to Persian.","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
7,Kurdish Music and Dance: A Deep Dive into Historical Roots,https://www.kurdish-history.com/post/kurdish-music-and-dance-a-deep-dive-into-historical-roots,"Kurdish music and dance are vital elements of Kurdistan culture and heritage, encapsulating the essence of the Kurdish people's journey through history. The vibrant rhythms and spirited moves tell stories etched in time, connecting generations with their kurdish history and imbuing each performance with cultural significance. This blog post explores the fascinating historical roots of ...","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
8,Kurdish Music - YouTube,https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLnOSH5j1sQh9_SiMbTOV6409FPLz0wj_3,"The Kurdish people live in northern Iraq, western Iran, Turkey and Syria. See also my Iraqi Music playlist as well as my Persian Music list.","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
9,The Kurdish Music - Institutkurde.org,https://www.institutkurde.org/en/kurdorama/music/,"The Kurdish Music The Kurdish Music In the cultural life of the Kurds, split up as they were in ancient times by feudal barriers, today by State frontiers, music came to play the role of a privileged, let us say unique medium: it filled a precise and basic social function. From historical chronicles to lyric poetry and from epics to literary works, all are sung, everything is put to music in ...","Kurdish, Northern",Indo-European
